WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.360
I used to get a lot of
questions about oil pulling.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.520
So back in 2017 I did
a video series on it.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.040
This video combines two
of those videos into one

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.840
looking at oil pulling
for oral health.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.640
See the Doctor's Notes for the
other two videos in the series

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.440
and stay tuned for news
of an upcoming webinar

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.400
I'm going to be doing on bad breath
and the effects of tongue scraping.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.040
Now to the video.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.417
Flashback Friday: Oil Pulling Benefits for
Plaque, Gingivitis, Teeth Whitening and Bad Breath Tested

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.160
What are the potential risks and
benefits of the ancient practice

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.760
of oil pulling, in which
oil is swished or pulled

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.840
between the teeth for like
15 minutes and then spit out?

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.280
There are wild, unsubstantiated
claims online,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.880
and even in the medical literature.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.440
I can believe it's good exercise for
your tongue and cheek muscles,

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.720
but healing all organs simultaneously?

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.920
That statement seems a bit of a tongue
and cheek exercise right there!

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.720
Respondents in the British Medical
Journal expressed surprise

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.360
reading that swishing some
oil around in the mouth

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.280
could effectively treat diseases
like meningitis, heart, kidney,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.120
hormone disorders. Oh, did
I mention cancer, AIDS, etc.?

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.240
Look, it may help in some way,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.520
but for things to enter into
evidence-based medicine,

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.880
we actually need a little
something called evidence,

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.320
without being distracted by illusory
effects ascribed by their advocates,

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.480
especially when another
set of respondents

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.040
shared their experience of a case
of severe inflammatory gingivitis

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.680
that worsened after oil pulling.
She stopped and it got better.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:45.760
They were thinking
maybe some of the oil

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.520
got like stuck under her gums.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.800
Regardless, maybe
people should hold off

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.840
until we actually have
some evidence.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.880
Thankfully, there's a Center for
Evidence-Based Medicine at Oxford

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.760
that recently compiled
all the controlled trials.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.800
Why is it so important
there's a control group?

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.960
Because maybe the reported
declines in gingivitis and plaques

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.640
with oil pulling are just because
in the study they had dentists

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.280
looking over their shoulders
with constant check-ups.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.120
So maybe the study subjects just
upped their brushing and flossing game.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.640
This is the kind of study we need.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.320
No changes before and
after in the control group,

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.280
but a drop in plaque and gingivitis
scores in the oil pulling group.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.040
That's more like it.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.840
OK, so there does
seem to be an effect,

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.720
but what do those numbers mean?

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.000
When you test a new
drug, it's not enough

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.520
to show it works better than
nothing, better than a sugar pill.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.960
What you ideally want to know
is does the new drug work

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.480
better than the current best drug
out there, for the same condition.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.360
Otherwise, what's the
point of a new therapy?

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
That's why drug companies are often
forced to use so-called active controls,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.200
compare their drug not just
compared to nothing, a placebo,

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.280
but head-to-head against
the leading drug.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.040
Here, oil pulling is
compared to doing nothing.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.280
How about oil pulling
compared to chlorhexidine,

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.720
an antiseptic chemical used
in medicated mouthwash,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.520
which is considered
to be a gold standard

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.880
in the fight against plaque,
cavities, and gingivitis?

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.520
So here we go.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.080
Ladies and gentlemen, in one
corner, we have oil pulling.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.520
In the other, chlorhexidine,
measuring their ability

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.560
to lower the number of
cavity-producing bacteria

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.480
on people's teeth, and...

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.440
Chlorhexidine worked faster,
a significant drop within just 24 hours

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.720
whereas, it took a week for the
oil pulling to really start working.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.920
But by two weeks, the oil
pulling may end up

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.640
just as efficacious
as the gold standard.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.720
In fact, they appeared
to work so similarly

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.880
skeptical me is critically
thinking, "Wait a second,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.440
maybe it's just the physical act of
swishing that disrupts the plaque?"

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.360
It would have been cool if
they included a third group

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.080
that just swished with
water, and they did!

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.000
And swishing with
water had no effect.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.280
Now, it wasn't exactly fair.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.520
They had the oil pulling
group swishing for 10 minutes,

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:19.680
whereas the other two groups
only swished for one.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.120
So for all we know, swishing
with water for 10 minutes

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:24.560
might be as good as the oil.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.920
And one could look at this
and argue that chlorhexidine

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.400
actually worked 10 times
better, since it got

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.120
the same effect swishing
for one-tenth the time.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.280
But chlorhexidine has side effects,
potentially serious side effects,

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.320
like painful desquamation, meaning
peeling of the mucous membranes

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:47.840
in your mouth, as well as
discoloration of the teeth or tongue.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.080
So oil has certain benefits over
commercially available mouth rinses:

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.760
it's non-chemical, non-alcoholic,
low cost, and non-staining.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:57.840
Yet the effectiveness is unclear.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.680
But wait, oil pulling has been
shown to significantly drop

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.920
the number of cavity-causing
bacteria on the teeth.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:10.320
Yeah, but does that translate
out into actual fewer cavities?

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:14.400
That's what we really care about,
but it hasn't been studied.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.040
They did pair up oil pulling
versus chlorhexidine

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.480
against plaque and gingivitis,

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.600
and did find they were both
able to help to a similar degree,

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.120
and plaque-induced gingivitis
is a reasonable predictor

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:29.280
of future dental health, thereby
explaining the Oxford Center's

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.320
conclusion that oil pulling may indeed

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:35.600
have beneficial effects
on oral and dental health.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.360
A review of the effects of oil pulling
concluded that the ancient practice

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.720
may indeed have beneficial effects
on oral and dental health.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.320
I have talked about the benefits for
dental health, but oral health, too?

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.880
Oil pulling was also tested
against "oral malodor,"

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.760
also known as halitosis,
or simply, bad breath.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:59.680
It's believed a quarter of the
world's population suffers from it.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.600
So they decided to put oil pulling
versus chlorhexidine to the test.

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.440
How do you test for bad breath, though?

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.280
There's all sorts of really fancy
methods—gas chromatography,

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.880
electronic noses, diamond probes,
and dark field microscopy—

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.080
but these are really expensive,
or not very reliable.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:21.120
So they decided to go
with the "gold standard."

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:26.000
Study subjects were just told
to breath in the researcher's face.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.200
And then they wanted to know
what the study subjects thought

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.800
about their own breath, so they
asked them to lick their own wrist

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.280
and sniff it, then give it a score
from zero to extremely foul.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.840
And though the subjects themselves
thought their licked wrists

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.320
smelled better after
two weeks of oil puling,

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.640
the researchers disagreed that
their breath smelled any better.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:53.440
But after three weeks there was
a significant and comparable

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:57.360
improvement in breath odor in the
oil pulling and chlorhexidine groups.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.800
I was excited about this study
because they used an actual placebo,

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.400
colored water, to presumably match
the look of the chlorhexidine,

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.360
and swishing for the same duration.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.640
Aha! So we can finally answer
that nagging question

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.000
about whether oil pulling cuts
down on plaque and gingivitis

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.560
because of the oil, or just because

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.640
you're swishing anything
in your mouth that long.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.120
And the water worked just
as well—the same drop

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.160
swishing with oil or just
swishing with water—

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.200
suggesting that the
plaque is just disrupted

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:34.960
by the extended rinsing action.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.960
Yes, oil may be 5 or 6 times cheaper
than chlorhexidine and safer,

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.960
but cheaper and safer
than just plain water?

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.640
Yeah, but can water whiten teeth?

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.440
Numerous websites offer testimonials

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.360
of oil pulling whitening teeth, but
there were no studies published

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.120
in the medical literature, and so most
doctors would just give up there.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.920
But these two dental professors
in Detroit decided to put it to the test.

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:07.200
Teeth were selected from
their stored collection

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.360
of human extracted teeth
(sounds a little horror movie-ish).

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.720
Then they put them in tubes
with coconut oil, sesame oil,

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:19.360
sunflower oil, along with some
fake saliva and vigorously shake

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.000
them every day for two weeks
and found... no evidence

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.400
to suggest that oil pulling has
any effect on teeth whitening.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.520
That's like when another internet
darling was put to the test,

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.600
strawberries and baking soda, which
was evidently featured on Dr. Oz.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:41.520
Over-the-counter whitening strips worked;
a home whitening system works,

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.480
where the dentist sends you
home with custom trays;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.280
and in-office tooth
whitening works,

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:52.160
but the DIY strawberry
baking soda mixture failed,

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.440
as bad as just plain water
used as a control.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:00.080
What about dental erosion?

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:02.880
In my video on plant-based
diets and dental health,

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:07.120
I talked about how those eating
healthier may have healthier gums,

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:09.200
but because they tend
to eat more acidic foods

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.760
like citrus and tomatoes, fruity teas,

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.120
they may be at more risk for
eroding off some of their enamel,

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:18.000
which is why we should
rinse our mouth with water

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:21.120
after eating or drinking
anything acidic, anything sour,

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:25.200
but what about rinsing
with oil every morning?

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:28.800
The way our body protects
our teeth from erosion

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:33.760
is by forming a "pellicle" over
our teeth, a protective layer

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:37.440
of mostly proteins from
our saliva, but some fat, too.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:41.840
So, hey, might oil pulling
help prevent erosive damage

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:45.520
to our tooth surfaces by kind of
buttressing this protective layer?

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:49.840
You don't know until
you put it to the test.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:54.880
Now they wanted to put the teeth
under a microscope afterwards,

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:57.360
and that's hard to do when
they're still in people's heads.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:01.360
So they put slabs of cattle
teeth in their mouth,

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:05.600
let them sit there until that
protective layer developed,

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:09.680
and then oil pulled around the
teeth—or not in the control group—

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:12.640
and then took them out
and exposed them to acid.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.200
If you expose the teeth to acid
without putting them in your mouth,

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.320
within 2 minutes, 120 seconds,
significant demineralization takes place.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.280
Calcium is dissolved out
of the teeth by the acid.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.840
But just let those same teeth roll around
in your mouth for a few minutes

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:32.640
and then expose them to
acid, there's less erosion.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:37.360
OK, but then what happens if you put them
in your mouth and do some oil pulling?

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:41.600
Is there even less erosion?
No, there's more.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:45.840
It's as if the oil pulling
undermined the protective layer.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:50.560
And that's exactly what they
saw under the microscope.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.320
Here's what that
protective layer looks like

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.920
before the oil pulling and then after.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:58.720
They suspect the oil may actually

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:13.840
be depleting the protective
layer of some of its protection.

